  <refsection>
    <title>Description</title>
    <para>
      A <classname>SmallSystem</classname> object represents a small
      part of the linearized system that is constructed when the
      finite element matrices are built.  The
      <classname>SmallSystem</classname> facilitates the insertion of
      information calculated by a &Property; into the finite element
      vectors and matrices. An &Equation; or &Flux; creates a
      <classname>SmallSystem</classname>, passes it to a &Property;,
      and then uses the values from the
      <classname>SmallSystem</classname> to fill in the global finite
      element vectors and matrices.  The &Property; needs to know only
      local information, such as the current &Element; and &Node; and
      &Field; and &Flux; components.
    </para>
    <para>
      Generally, in C++ <classname>SmallSystem</classname>s appear in
      expressions like

      <programlisting>
smallsystem-><emphasis role="bold">method</emphasis>(i, field, j, node) += floating_point_value; </programlisting>

      or

      <programlisting>
smallsystem-><emphasis role="bold">method</emphasis>() += object; </programlisting>

      where <methodname>method</methodname> is one of the methods
      described below.  <methodname>method</methodname> returns a
      reference to the object in which the right hand side value
      should be stored.  Note that the line
      <emphasis>increments</emphasis> the referenced value, and does
      not simply set it.  Another &Property; may have already
      contributed to the values being computed, and its data should
      not be overwritten.
    </para>
    <para>
      In Python, the syntax is different.  Instead of
      <programlisting>
smallsystem->stiffness_matrix_element(i, displacement, j, node) += <emphasis role="bold">value</emphasis>; </programlisting>
      for example, use
      <programlisting>
smallsystem.<emphasis role="bold">add</emphasis>_stiffness_matrix_element(i, displacement, j, node, <emphasis role="bold">value</emphasis>);</programlisting>
      
    </para>
  </refsection>               <!-- SmallSystem description -->
  
  <refsection>
    <title>Methods</title>
    <refsection id="Class-SmallSystem-stiffness_matrix_element">
      <title><code>stiffness_matrix_element</code> and <code>add_stiffness_matrix_element</code></title>
      <para>
        <methodname>stiffness_matrix_element</methodname> is used to
        make a <link
        linkend="Class-FluxProperty"><classname>FluxProperty</classname>'s</link>
        contribution to a finite element stiffness matrix. It is
        called by the <link
        linkend="Class-FluxProperty-flux_matrix"><methodname>flux_matrix</methodname></link>
        method of the &Property;.  See <link
        linkend="Class-FluxProperty-flux_matrix"><methodname>flux_matrix</methodname></link>
        for the full explanation and an example.
      </para>
      <para>
        There are two C++ variations of
        <methodname>stiffness_matrix_element</methodname> for scalar and non-scalar
        &Fields;:
        <programlisting>
double&amp; stiffness_matrix_element(const FieldIndex&amp; flux_component,
                                     const Field* field,
                                     const FieldIndex&amp; field_component,
                                     const ElementFuncNodeIterator&amp; node);
double&amp; stiffness_matrix_element(const FieldIndex&amp; flux_component,
                                     const Field* field,
                                     const ElementFuncNodeIterator&amp; node); </programlisting>

        The arguments are:
        <variablelist>
          <varlistentry>
            <term><code>const <link
            linkend="Class-FieldIndex">FieldIndex&amp;</link> flux_component</code></term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                The component of the &Flux; being computed. 
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term><code>const &Field;* field</code></term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                The &Field; making the contribution to the &Flux;.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term><code>const <link
            linkend="Class-FieldIndex">FieldIndex&amp;</link>
            field_component</code>
            </term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                The component of the &Field;.  This argument should
                be omitted if the &Field; is a <link
                linkend="Class-ScalarField"><classname>ScalarField</classname></link>.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term>const <link
            linkend="Class-ElementFuncNodeIterator">ElementFuncNodeIterator</link>&amp;
            node</term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                A pointer to the current &Node; of the &Element;
                being examined.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
        </variablelist>
      </para>
      <para>
        In Python,
        <programlisting>
smallsystem.add_stiffness_matrix_element(i, field, j, node, increment) </programlisting>
        is equivalent to calling
        <programlisting>
smallsystem->stiffness_matrix_element(i, field, j, node) += increment; </programlisting>
        in C++.
      </para>
    </refsection> <!--SmallSystem::stiffness_matrix_element -->

    <!-- SmallSystem::damping_matrix_element -->
    <refsection id="Class-SmallSystem-damping_matrix_element">
      <title><code>damping_matrix_element</code> and
      <code>add_damping_matrix_element</code></title>

      <para>
        <methodname>damping_matrix_element</methodname> is called by
        both <link
        linkend="Class-EqnProperty"><classname>EqnProperty</classname>s</link>
        and <link
        linkend="Class-FluxProperty"><classname>FluxProperty</classname>s</link>.
        It's used by an <link
        linkend="Class-EqnProperty"><classname>EqnProperty</classname></link>'s
        <link
            linkend="Class-EqnProperty-time_deriv_matrices"><methodname>time_deriv_matrices</methodname></link>
        method to make contributions to a the matrix that multiplies
        the time derivatives of the &Fields; (Eqn. <xref
        linkend="Eqn-EqnProperty-C"/>) in a force balance equation.
        See <link
        linkend="Class-EqnProperty-time_deriv_matrices"><methodname>time_deriv_matrices</methodname></link>
        for more details and an example.
      </para>
      <para>
        <methodname>damping_matrix_element</methodname> is also used
        by a <link
        linkend="Class-FluxProperty"><classname>FluxProperty</classname></link>'s
        <link
            linkend="Class-FluxProperty-flux_matrix"><methodname>flux_matrix</methodname></link> method
        to make contributions to the matrix
        <inlineequation>
          <alt role="tex">
            \({\bf C}_{ik\nu}\)
          </alt>
          </inlineequation> that multiplies time derivatives of the
          fields in Eqn. <xref linkend="Eqn-damping-matrix"/>.  See <link
          linkend="Class-FluxProperty-flux_matrix"><methodname>flux_matrix</methodname></link>
          for more details and an example.
      </para>
      <para>
        There are two variations of
        <methodname>damping_matrix_element</methodname>, for scalar
        and non-scalar &Fields;:
        <programlisting>
double&amp; damping_matrix_element(const FieldIndex&amp; component,
                                   const Field* field,
                                   const FieldIndex&amp; field_component,
                                   const ElementFuncNodeIterator&amp; node);
double&amp; damping_matrix_element(const FieldIndex&amp; component,
                                   const Field* field,
                                   const ElementFuncNodeIterator&amp; node); </programlisting>

        The arguments are:
        <variablelist>
          <varlistentry>
            <term><code>const <link
            linkend="Class-FieldIndex">FieldIndex&amp;</link> component</code></term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                If being used in an <link
                linkend="Class-EqnProperty"><classname>EqnProperty</classname>s</link>,
                <varname>component</varname> is the index of a
                component of the &Equation;.  If being used in a <link
                linkend="Class-FluxProperty"><classname>FluxProperty</classname>s</link>,
                it's the index of a component of the &Flux;.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term><code>const &Field;* field</code></term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                The &Field; making the contribution to the &Equation;.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term><code>const <link
            linkend="Class-FieldIndex">FieldIndex&amp;</link>
            field_component</code>
            </term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                The component of the &Field;.  This argument should
                be omitted if the &Field; is a <link
                linkend="Class-ScalarField"><classname>ScalarField</classname></link>.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term>const <link
            linkend="Class-ElementFuncNodeIterator">ElementFuncNodeIterator</link>&amp;
            node</term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                A pointer to the current &Node; of the &Element;
                being examined.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
        </variablelist>          
      </para>
      <para>
        In Python,
        <programlisting>
smallsystem.add_damping_matrix_element(i, field, j, node, increment) </programlisting>
        is equivalent to calling
        <programlisting>
smallsystem->damping_matrix_element(i, field, j, node) += increment; </programlisting>
        in C++.
      </para>
    </refsection>      <!-- SmallSystem::damping_matrix_element -->
    
    <!-- SmallSystem::mass_matrix_element -->
    <refsection id="Class-SmallSystem-mass_matrix_element">
      <title><code>mass_matrix_element</code> and <code>add_mass_matrix_element</code></title>
      <para>
        <methodname>mass_matrix_element</methodname> is called by an
        <link
        linkend="Class-EqnProperty"><classname>EqnProperty</classname></link>
        to make contributions to a mass matrix, from its <link
        linkend="Class-EqnProperty-time_deriv_matrices"><methodname>time_deriv_matrices</methodname></link>
        method.  The mass matrix multiplies the second time
        derivatives of the &Fields; in a force balance equation.  See
        <link
        linkend="Class-EqnProperty-time_deriv_matrices"><methodname>time_deriv_matrices</methodname></link>
        for more details and an example.
      </para>
      <para>
        There are two variations of
        <methodname>mass_matrix_element</methodname>, forscalar and
        non-scalar &Fields;:
        
        <programlisting>
double&amp; mass_matrix_element(const FieldIndex&amp; eqncomp,
                                const Field* field,
                                const FieldIndex&amp; field_component,
                                const ElementFuncNodeIterator&amp; node);
double&amp; mass_matrix_element(const FieldIndex&amp; eqncomp,
                                const Field* field,
                                const ElementFuncNodeIterator&amp; node); </programlisting>

        The arguments are:
        <variablelist>
          <varlistentry>
            <term><code>const <link
            linkend="Class-FieldIndex">FieldIndex&amp;</link> eqncomp</code></term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                The component of the &Equation; being computed, obtained
                by iterating over the components in
                <methodname>time_deriv_matrices</methodname>. 
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term><code>const &Field;* field</code></term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                The &Field; making the contribution to the &Equation;.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term><code>const <link
            linkend="Class-FieldIndex">FieldIndex&amp;</link>
            field_component</code>
            </term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                The component of the &Field;.  This argument should
                be omitted if the &Field; is a <link
                linkend="Class-ScalarField"><classname>ScalarField</classname></link>.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term>const <link
            linkend="Class-ElementFuncNodeIterator">ElementFuncNodeIterator</link>&amp;
            node</term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                A pointer to the current &Node; of the &Element;
                being examined.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
        </variablelist>          
      </para>
      <para>
        In Python,
        <programlisting>
smallsystem.add_mass_matrix_element(i, eqn, j, node, increment) </programlisting>
        is equivalent to calling
        <programlisting>
smallsystem->mass_matrix_element(i, eqn, j, node) += increment; </programlisting>
        in C++.
      </para>
    </refsection>      <!-- SmallSystem::mass_matrix_element -->

    <!-- SmallSystem::force_deriv_matrix_element -->
    <refsection id="Class-SmallSystem-force_deriv_matrix_element">
      <title><code>force_deriv_matrix_element</code> and <code>add_force_deriv_matrix_element</code></title>
      <para>
        <methodname>force_deriv_matrix_element</methodname> is used
        to make an nonlinear <link
        linkend="Class-EqnProperty"><classname>EqnProperty</classname>'s</link>
        contribution to force derivatives.  It is called by <link
        linkend="Class-EqnProperty-force_deriv_matrix"><methodname>force_deriv_matrix</methodname></link>
        of the &Property;. See <link
        linkend="Class-EqnProperty-force_deriv_matrix"><methodname>force_deriv_matrix</methodname></link>
        for the details and an example.
      </para>
      <para>
        There are two variations of
        <methodname>force_deriv_matrix_element</methodname>, for const
        and non-const stiffness matrices, and scalar and non-scalar
        &Fields;:
        <programlisting>
double&amp; force_deriv_matrix_element(const FieldIndex&amp; eqncomp,
                                       const Field* field,
                                       const FieldIndex&amp; field_component,
                                       const ElementFuncNodeIterator&amp; node);
double&amp; force_deriv_matrix_element(const FieldIndex&amp; eqncomp,
                                       const Field* field,
                                       const ElementFuncNodeIterator&amp; node); </programlisting>

        The arguments are:
        <variablelist>
          <varlistentry>
            <term><code>const <link
            linkend="Class-FieldIndex">FieldIndex&amp;</link> eqncomp</code></term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                The component of the &Equation; being computed. 
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term><code>const &Field;* field</code></term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                The &Field; making the contribution to the &Equation;.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term><code>const <link
            linkend="Class-FieldIndex">FieldIndex&amp;</link>
            field_component</code>
            </term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                The component of the &Field;.  This argument should
                be omitted if the &Field; is a <link
                linkend="Class-ScalarField"><classname>ScalarField</classname></link>.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term>const <link
            linkend="Class-ElementFuncNodeIterator">ElementFuncNodeIterator</link>&amp;
            node</term>
            <listitem>
              <para>
                A pointer to the current &Node; of the &Element;
                being examined.
              </para>
            </listitem>
          </varlistentry>
        </variablelist>          
      </para>
      <para>
        In Python,
        <programlisting>
smallsystem.add_force_deriv_matrix_element(i, eqn, j, node, increment) </programlisting>
        is equivalent to calling
        <programlisting>
smallsystem->force_deriv_matrix_element(i, eqn, j, node) += increment; </programlisting>
        in C++.
      </para>
    </refsection>      <!-- SmallSystem::force_deriv_matrix_element -->

    <refsection id="Class-SmallSystem-fluxVector">
      <title>
        <code>fluxVector</code>, <code>flux_vector_element</code>, and
        <code>add_flux_vector-element</code>
      </title>
      <para>
        <methodname>fluxVector</methodname> and
        <methodname>flux_vector_element</methodname> can called by
        <link
            linkend="Class-FluxProperty-flux_value"><methodname>FluxProperty::flux_value</methodname></link>
        to get access to the components of a &Flux; either as a
        vector or individually.  Use
        <methodname>fluxVector</methodname> to set all components at
        once:
        <programlisting>
smallsystem->fluxVector() += vector_of_values;
        </programlisting>
        or <methodname>flux_vector_element</methodname> to set one
        at at time:
        <programlisting>
for(auto index: flux.components())
    smallsystem->flux_vector_element(index) += value; </programlisting>

        See <link
        linkend="Class-FluxProperty-flux_value"><methodname>FluxProperty::flux_value</methodname></link>
        for more details and an example.
      </para>
      <para>
        <methodname>fluxVector</methodname> takes no arguments.
        The argument to <methodname>flux_vector_element</methodname>
        can be either an integer index or a &FieldIndex; indicating
        which &Flux; component is being set.  The &Flux; values are
        stored as a vector even if the &Flux; is a tensor, but the
        &FieldIndex; class knows how to find the correct integer
        index, so use &FieldIndex; when in doubt.
      </para>
      <para>
        In Python,
        <programlisting>
smallsystem.add_flux_vector-element(i, value) </programlisting>
        is equivalent to calling
        <programlisting>
smallsystem->flux_vector-element(i) += value; </programlisting>
        in C++.
      </para>
      
    </refsection>             <!-- SmallSystem::fluxVector -->

    <refsection id="Class-SmallSystem-forceVector">
      <title>
        <code>forceVector</code>, <code>force_vector_element</code>,
        and <code>add_force_vector-element</code>
      </title>
      <para>
        <methodname>forceVector</methodname> and
        <methodname>force_vector_element</methodname> can be called by
        <link
        linkend="Class-EqnProperty-force_value"><code>EqnProperty::force_value</code></link>
        to make contributions to the force term in Eq. <xref
        linkend="Eqn-TimeDivergence"/>.
        Use <methodname>forceVector</methodname> to set all
        components<footnote><simpara>There is one force component for
        each component of the &Equation;.</simpara></footnote> at once:
        <programlisting>
smallsystem->forceVector() += vector_of_values; </programlisting>
        or <methodname>force_vector_element</methodname> to set one
        at at time:
        <programlisting>
for(auto index: *equation->components())
    smallsystem->forceVector(index) += value; </programlisting>
        See <link
        linkend="Class-EqnProperty-force_value"><code>EqnProperty::force_value</code></link>
        for an example.
      </para>
      <para>
        <methodname>forceVector</methodname> takes no arguments.
        The argument to <methodname>force_vector_element</methodname>
        can be either an integer index or a &FieldIndex; indicating
        which force component is being set.  The force values are
        stored as a vector even if the force is a tensor, but the
        &FieldIndex; class knows how to find the correct integer
        index, so use &FieldIndex; when in doubt.
      </para>
      <para>
        In Python,
        <programlisting>
smallsystem.add_force_vector-element(i, value) </programlisting>
        is equivalent to calling
        <programlisting>
smallsystem->force_vector-element(i) += value; </programlisting>
        in C++.
      </para>
    </refsection>               <!-- SmallSystem::forceVector -->

    <refsection id="Class-SmallSystem-offsetVector">
      <title>
        <code>offsetVector</code>, <code>offset_vector_element</code>,
        and <code>add_offset_vector-element</code>
      </title>
      <para>
        A <link
        linkend="Class-FluxProperty"><classname>FluxProperty</classname></link>
        that computes a &Flux; whose linearization includes a
        field-independent part (&sigma0; in Eqn. <xref
        linkend="Eqn-flux1"/>) will define <link
        linkend="Class-FluxProperty-flux_offset"><code>FluxProperty::flux_offset</code></link>
        to compute the constant.
        <methodname>flux_offset</methodname> should call
        <code>SmallSystem::offsetVector</code> or
        <code>SmallSystem::offset_vector_element</code> to store the
        offset.  Use <methodname>offsetVector</methodname> to set
        all the components at once:
        
        <programlisting>
smallsystem->offsetVector() += vector_of_values; </programlisting>
        or <methodname>offset_vector_element()</methodname> to store one
        at a time:
        
        <programlisting>
for(auto index: *flux->components())
    smallsystem->offset_vector_element(index) += value; </programlisting>
      </para>
      <para>
        <methodname>offsetValue</methodname> takes no arguments.
        The argument to
        <methodname>offset_vector_element</methodname> can be either
        an integer index or a &FieldIndex; indicating which flux
        component is being set.  The flux values are stored as a
        vector even if the flux is a tensor, but the &FieldIndex;
        class knows how to find the correct integer index, so use
        &FieldIndex; when in doubt.
      </para>
      <para>
        For more details and an example, see <link
        linkend="Class-FluxProperty-flux_offset"><methodname>FluxProperty::flux_offset</methodname></link>.
      </para>
      <para>
        In Python,
        <programlisting>
smallsystem.add_offset_vector-element(i, value) </programlisting>
        is equivalent to calling
        <programlisting>
smallsystem->offset_vector-element(i) += value; </programlisting>
        in C++.
      </para>
    </refsection>               <!-- SmallSystem::offsetVector -->
    
  </refsection>        <!-- SmallSystem methods -->
</refentry>
